issue(id="0013" status="review")
  title Define structural extraction policy for HTML import
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of the puggers HTML converter
    i-want structural simplification choices to be explicit extraction policy rather than loosely accumulated heuristics
    so-that users can trust when wrappers, shells, and surrounding regions are removed versus preserved
  premise.
    The converter is intentionally allowed to suppress noise, but its current
    structural controls are still coarse: `trim_outer_document` is broad,
    `collapse-single-nested` is heuristic, and there is no clear model for when
    surrounding structure is considered framing noise versus meaningful signal.
    This deserves its own task rather than staying mixed into baseline
    correction work. The output here should be a deliberate policy for wrapper
    removal and region extraction, with concrete examples of what is safe to
    drop, what must be preserved, and what should require an explicit option.
    `collapse-single-nested` and richer region selection are distinct features,
    but they belong together here because both are really about deciding what
    structural context counts as noise.
    For single-child structural chains, `div` should not be treated as the
    preferred representative by default. A chain such as `div > section > div`
    should be able to collapse to `section`, which means the policy is broader
    than "remove anonymous `div` wrappers." This task should implement
    `collapse-single-nested` as one of four explicit modes: off, top wins,
    bottom wins, or best tag wins. The initial "best tag" ordering can live as a
    puggers-owned global constant, with future configuration left open if real
    users need to tune the hierarchy.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Revisit `collapse-single-nested` and classify at least a few concrete wrapper-removal cases as safe, unsafe, or option-dependent.
    criterion Implement single-child structural collapse as an explicit option with off, top-wins, bottom-wins, and best-tag-wins behavior, including a concrete example such as `div > section > div` collapsing to `section` when best-tag policy applies.
    criterion Define the initial best-tag hierarchy as an internal global policy while keeping the API open to future configuration.
    criterion Decide whether broader region-selection controls are needed beyond `trim_outer_document`, and either implement the minimal useful control set or document why the current boundary is sufficient for now.
    criterion Add focused tests for wrapper-heavy and shell-heavy HTML samples so structural extraction decisions are reproducible instead of anecdotal.
    criterion Make sure structural extraction policy is described independently from text-layout or formatter-compatibility concerns.
    criterion Leave the importer with a smaller, more intentional set of structural heuristics than it started with, even if that means narrowing behavior rather than adding more options.
  review-items
    item(status="covered") The anonymous wrapper collapse flag is now an explicit collapse mode with off, top-wins, bottom-wins, and best-tag-wins semantics.
    item(status="covered") The first best-tag hierarchy is defined globally inside puggers, while leaving custom hierarchy configuration as a possible future extension.
    item(status="covered") Front-matter and back-matter trimming stay out of scope for now; `trim_outer_document` remains a body-shell control rather than a broader region-selection proxy.
    item(status="covered") Structural simplification policy is documented separately from formatter compatibility so future CLI and npm surfaces can explain the option without inheriting formatter caveats.
